[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Relational Databases: Sharding, Partitioning, and Federation"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As applications scale and data volumes grow into terabytes or petabytes, a single relational database instance inevitably hits hardware limits regarding CPU, memory, I/O operations, and storage capacity. To maintain high availability and low latency, systems architects must scale databases horizontally or vertically. This tutorial explores three core architectural patterns for scaling relational databases: Partitioning, Federation, and Sharding."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Database Partitioning (Intra-Node Scaling)"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Partitioning involves splitting a single large logical table into smaller, more manageable physical pieces on the same database server or storage tier. The database engine manages these partitions transparently during query execution, a concept known as partition pruning."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Range Partitioning: Rows are distributed based on a range of values, such as dates or IDs (e.g., orders by year)."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"List Partitioning: Rows are grouped based on explicit lists of discrete values, such as geographic regions or department codes."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Hash Partitioning: A hashing algorithm is applied to a partition key to distribute rows evenly across a defined number of partitions, preventing hot spots."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"CREATE TABLE orders (
    order_id INT,
    order_date DATE,
    customer_id INT,
    amount DECIMAL(10,2)
)
PARTITION BY RANGE (YEAR(order_date)) (
    PARTITION p2021 VALUES LESS THAN (2022),
    PARTITION p2022 VALUES LESS THAN (2023),
    PARTITION p2023 VALUES LESS THAN (2024)
);"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Database Federation (Functional Decomposition)"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Federation, or functional partitioning, splits a monolithic database into multiple smaller databases based on business domains or service boundaries. Instead of having one database handle users, products, and transactions, federation assigns a dedicated database server to each distinct functional area."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"User Service connects exclusively to the User Database."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Order Service connects exclusively to the Order Database."},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"Federation reduces contention on shared resources and aligns well with microservices architectures. However, it introduces complexity when executing cross-domain queries or maintaining ACID transactions across disparate database instances, often requiring application-level orchestration or the Saga pattern."},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Database Sharding (Horizontal Scaling)"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Sharding is the process of horizontally scaling a relational database by breaking up a single large database schema and distributing its rows across multiple independent database servers (shards). Unlike partitioning, which can occur on a single machine, shards share nothing and operate across a network cluster."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Key-based (Hash-based) Sharding: Applying a hash function to a shard key (like user_id) to compute the target shard. Provides even distribution but makes schema migrations and range queries challenging."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Directory-based Sharding: Using a lookup service or catalog database to map keys to shards. Highly flexible, but the directory can become a single point of failure or a performance bottleneck."},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Range-based Sharding: Allocating specific ranges of the shard key to specific servers. Easy for range queries, but prone to hot spots if certain ranges receive more traffic."},{"order":19,"type":"code","text":"-- Conceptual routing logic in application layer
function getDatabaseConnection(userId) {
    const shardId = userId % 4;
    return connectionPools[shardId];
}"},{"order":20,"type":"subheading","text":"Architectural Trade-Offs and Challenges"},{"order":21,"type":"paragraph","text":"Implementing sharding, partitioning, or federation introduces significant operational overhead. Distributed joins become extremely expensive or impossible to execute natively. Enforcing unique constraints (like global email uniqueness) requires distributed locks or application-layer validation. Furthermore, rebalancing data when a shard runs out of space requires complex data migration scripts and careful downtime planning."},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"Conclusion: Start with vertical scaling and query optimization. Transition to database partitioning or federation when functional domains emerge or single-table sizes balloon. Reserve database sharding as an advanced pattern for when absolute throughput and dataset limits exceed the capacity of a single high-end database server."}]
